T2.2 logic kernels vs OpenCV -- bitwiseAnd / bitwiseOr / bitwiseXor / bitwiseNot
================================================================================

DENOMINATOR (ARCHITECTURE 10.3): cv::bitwise_and / _or / _xor / _not on the SAME
binary content stored as CV_8U {0,255} -- what a user runs today without binCV.
Verified per size, per operation: both sides produce the same set-pixel count
before anything is timed, and a disagreement now SKIPS that size and exits
non-zero rather than printing a table beneath a warning.

WHICH OpenCV, AND WHY THE QUESTION IS PART OF THE RESULT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two OpenCV 4.x installs on this machine, both configured Release, both dispatching
the same instruction sets. cv::bitwise_and / _or / _xor agree between them within
run-to-run noise. cv::bitwise_not DOES NOT: it runs at ~105 GB/s in the distro
4.5.4 build and ~32 GB/s in the locally built 4.8.0-dev one, a 3.3x gap in the
denominator alone.

Both sets of numbers are below. **The 4.5.4 column is the reported result**,
because it is the faster denominator and therefore the conservative one, and
because it is the package a user gets by installing OpenCV rather than building
it. The earlier version of this log reported 18-20x for bitwiseNot; that figure
was a property of one local OpenCV build and is WITHDRAWN. Against a denominator
that dispatches properly, bitwiseNot is 5.5x -- see READING IT point 3, which is
the least flattering number in this file and the most informative.

PLATFORM -- NOT AUTHORITATIVE for micro-decisions (EXPERIMENTS.md, "Measurement
platforms"). x86_64 desktop under WSL2, which can indicate a large algorithmic
effect but cannot close E-1/E-2/E-3.

  CPU        AMD Ryzen 5 5600X, 6 cores / 12 threads
             L1d 32 KiB per core, L2 512 KiB per core, L3 32 MiB shared
  Kernel     Linux 5.15.167.4-microsoft-standard-WSL2 x86_64
  Compiler   g++ (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04) 11.4.0, -O3 (CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release)
  OpenCV     REPORTED: 4.5.4 (distro), Baseline SSE SSE2, dispatch SSE4_1..AVX512_SKX
             ALSO RUN:  4.8.0-dev (/usr/local), Baseline SSE SSE2 SSE3, same dispatch
  Threads    cv::getNumThreads() = 1 in every run below. taskset to a single core
             leaves OpenCV one thread whatever its default would have been; an
             earlier version of this header recorded the UNPINNED default (12)
             while every raw run underneath it printed 1.
  Pinning    taskset -c 3; three whole runs per denominator, medians reported
  Governor   not available under WSL2; no thermal state is observable here either,
             which is one more reason this platform cannot close an experiment

REPRODUCE
  cmake -S bincv-cpp -B bincv-cpp/build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
  cmake --build bincv-cpp/build --target logic_benchmark -j
  taskset -c 3 ./bincv-cpp/build/benchmark/logic_benchmark

  The build picks up whichever OpenCV CMake finds. To pin the denominator
  explicitly, build the one file by hand:
    g++ -O3 -DNDEBUG -std=gnu++17 -DBINCV_WITH_OPENCV \
        -Ibincv-cpp/benchmark -Ibincv-cpp/include -isystem /usr/include/opencv4 \
        bincv-cpp/benchmark/logic_benchmark.cpp -o lb -lopencv_core
  The binary prints its OpenCV version AND that build's baseline/dispatch lines,
  so a reader can tell which denominator produced any given table.

METHOD, and the five validity hazards it answers (EXPERIMENTS.md, "Verify the
benchmark measures something") -- the full argument is in the header comment of
bincv-cpp/benchmark/logic_benchmark.cpp:
  - every timed iteration folds destination words into a `volatile` sink, and each
    result's popcount is printed, so a deleted loop would be visible twice
  - inputs rotate through four distinct random pairs, so nothing is foldable and
    no iteration can reuse the previous answer
  - a one-read-one-write word-copy loop at the same footprint is the physical
    bound. It is NOISY and now says so: it prints its slowest batch alongside its
    fastest, and the gap is large (e.g. 143.04 GB/s best against 55.05 GB/s worst
    at 38400 B). A row above 1.5x the fastest batch is flagged IMPLAUSIBLE; none
    is. The old threshold was 4x, which against a 100-140 GB/s probe needed
    400-560 GB/s to fire and could only ever have caught total elimination.
  - std::memcpy is printed for context and is NOT the bound: 133 GB/s at 38400 B,
    ~8 GB/s at 307200 B and 131072 B, then back UP to 21-28 GB/s at 33 MB. It is
    not monotonic in size, so the "glibc switches to non-temporal stores above
    ~128 KB" story that used to be told about it here does not fit the data and
    has been withdrawn as an explanation. The swing is real; its cause is not
    established, and a primitive that swings 16x across the sizes under test
    cannot bound them either way.
  - batch length is calibrated to ~20 ms per batch; the reported time is the
    MINIMUM over 9 batches, i.e. the run least disturbed by the scheduler

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
